<DocsPage id="permissions">

          <p>
            Every tool action is checked against a ruleset whose effect is <strong>allow</strong>, <strong>ask</strong> or <strong>deny</strong>. Rules from the agent, from the repository and from your saved answers are merged: deny wins, then ask, then allow, and anything with no matching rule defaults to ask. An agent with no permissions at all is denied everything rather than trusted.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you answer <strong>always</strong>, that allow is saved for the repository, so the same action is not asked again. Saved answers are per repository, and you can clear them.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            For unattended runs the terminal agent takes <code>--auto</code> (also spelled <code>--yolo</code> or <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code>). It auto-approves anything not explicitly denied — deny rules still hold. It is off by default, and it is the flag to think twice about.
          </p>

          <h2 id="project-trust">Repository trust</h2>
          <p>
            A repository's own <code>vector.json</code> and <code>.vector</code> folder (its agents, commands, modes,
            plugins, tools, skills, MCP servers, formatters and language servers) load only after you trust the
            repository, because they can run code on your computer. Until then Vector applies the repository's deny
            rules, and its run limits, loop pauses and sharing settings only where they are stricter than yours, so a
            cloned repository cannot raise your spend cap or switch loop pauses off.
          </p>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.109, the app asks once, the first time you open a repository that has settings of its
            own: <strong>Trust this repository?</strong> Choose <strong>Trust this repository</strong> to load its
            settings as they are now, or <strong>Not now</strong> to leave them off, and the app does not ask about it
            again. Every such repository asks, including one you clone with <strong>Open from GitHub</strong>, because
            a clone can come from anyone's link. To trust one later, choose <strong>Trust this repository…</strong> in
            its menu on Home, or open Settings, <strong>Repository trust</strong>. In desktop 1.99.108 the same
            approval is the button in the session header. The TUI's <code>/trust</code> and the terminal's
            <code>vector trust status</code> and <code>vector trust approve</code> arrive with the next CLI release; the
            CLI that npm and <code>vectordev.ai/install</code> serve today has neither.
          </p>
          <p>
            The language servers and formatters you turn on in your own settings also stay off in a repository you have
            not trusted, because they run its code, and your own local MCP servers start in an empty folder outside it,
            so the repository's <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.npmrc</code> or Python modules cannot take them over.
            This holds in a repository with no settings of its own too. The app does not ask about such a repository;
            while your tools are held back there it shows a <strong>Repository not trusted</strong> notice, and you
            trust it in Settings, <strong>Repository trust</strong>. Agent and parallel workspaces are folders of their
            own with their own trust, so from one of their sessions Settings shows that folder beside the repository it
            came from.
          </p>
          <p>
            Trust covers the repository as it was when you approved it. A later change to anything that can run, from a
            pull or an edit, needs approval again, and the app asks once more; plans, memory and rules that Vector
            writes into <code>.vector</code> do not. Integrations you add from the app are kept with your own settings
            rather than in the repository, so they stay after a restart. A trusted repository can add allow rules, but
            it cannot lift a deny in your own settings.
          </p>

          <h2 id="read-only-specialists">Read-only specialists</h2>
          <p>
            From the next release, the read-only specialists (explore, review and security) ask before they run a test
            or build command such as <code>bun test</code>, <code>npm test</code>, <code>pytest</code>,
            <code>cargo test</code> or <code>go test</code>, because those run the repository's own code. Approve once,
            or approve always for the rest of the session. In an unattended <code>vector run</code> they are refused
            unless you pass <code>--auto</code>, and git and pull request reads still need no approval.
          </p>

          <h2 id="private-network">Your own network</h2>
          <p>
            From the next release, when an agent's web fetch would reach your own computer or network (a dev server on
            <code>localhost</code>, an intranet page, a cloud metadata address), Vector asks for
            <code>private_network</code> as well as <code>webfetch</code>, so a hostile page cannot steer the agent
            into your network. Approve it once for that address, or allow it in your settings, for example
            <code>"permission": {"{"} "private_network": {"{"} "http://localhost:3000": "allow" {"}"} {"}"}</code>.
            Addresses you put in your own settings (remote instructions, skill catalogs, providers such as a LAN Ollama
            host, MCP servers, references) are reached without asking.
          </p>

          <h2 id="shell-sandbox">The shell sandbox</h2>
          <p>
            Commands an agent runs can be confined to their workspace by the operating system's own mechanism — a
            seatbelt profile on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux where it is installed — by setting
            <code>VECTOR_SHELL_SANDBOX</code> from 1.99.99. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop
            1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read that name, so setting it there confines nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            It is opt-in and off by default, and Windows ships no equivalent, so on Windows Vector reports that it is
            running unconfined instead of implying protection it cannot provide.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
